{"connection_id": "BC-73e791bc302e13c7a34a", "ref": "WLC:Deut.2.7", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "חסר; lacked nothing in the wilderness", "note": "The commentary glosses khusr as perishing; Deuteronomy says Israel lacked nothing during forty years with God in the wilderness, giving the חסר root a concrete opposite of deprivation (table sound correspondence, unverified)."}
{"connection_id": "BC-abee9b738deb1266998c", "ref": "WLC:Deut.2.7", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "חסר / خ س ر; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's destructive loss contrasts with life in divine security; Moses says Israel lacked nothing through the wilderness years, using Hebrew חסר in a provision setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c99c8101e7b6ff208707", "ref": "WLC:Deut.23.16", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "do not return escaped slave", "note": "The commentary's asylum means protecting someone who has fled danger; the law forbids handing an escaped slave back to his master."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9d358ce99a4e8e6692cd", "ref": "WLC:Deut.23.17", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fugitive may dwell where safe", "note": "The commentary calls a secure dwelling the destination of asylum; the escaped slave may live among Israel wherever he chooses without oppression."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e5a21e1d7f9ee1a4f8b6", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.36", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "עצר / ع ص ر; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary derives a shelter sense from its source label for ع ص ر; this verse says God sees his people depleted until none remain, whether restrained or free, using Hebrew עצר for confinement in the opposite situation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2a50651119c6780c8a25", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.37", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "other gods as false rock", "note": "The commentary contrasts real asylum with a false mountain or spider shelter; God asks where the gods are whom Israel treated as its rock."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eaf46cc2b263d2beae2b", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.38", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "let false gods be refuge", "note": "The commentary says a refuge can prove unable to save; this taunt challenges the rival gods to rise and become a shelter to their worshippers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e246cb86083a26f303fa", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.39", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no rescuer from divine hand", "note": "The commentary's failed mountain cannot save against God's command; God declares no one can deliver from his hand, defining the limit of competing refuges."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3f2524bcbd9532f4be78", "ref": "WLC:Deut.33.27", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "dwelling place and everlasting arms", "note": "The commentary develops refuge and armor as shelter; Moses calls the eternal God Israel's dwelling place with everlasting arms beneath, combining a safe home and protective arms."}
{"connection_id": "BC-47bd59fdad5b6d654496", "ref": "WLC:Deut.33.27", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God refuge, arms beneath", "note": "The commentary's clinging person needs support against destruction; Israel's blessing puts everlasting arms underneath and calls God its dwelling refuge."}

