{"connection_id": "BC-5e9f0f8356455dfe0e0e", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.5", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "promised descendants under insecurity", "note": "The commentary calls lineage a place one might turn for security; God promises the childless Abram uncountable descendants before Abram's act of faith."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7babc441fe8465a1353f", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.6", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "אמנ; Abraham trusts YHWH", "note": "The commentary identifies believers as those who enter safety; Genesis says Abraham trusted YHWH and was reckoned righteous, connecting trust with a standing described as righteous (the root match is a table sound correspondence)."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8adeca82ffc0bb2f03b8", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.6", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "אמנ / ء م ن; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary calls believers people entering security; Abram believes God's promise of descendants, and the Hebrew אמנ root makes trust the response to a threatened lineage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3181fcec9b2b4a4305b1", "ref": "WLC:Gen.19.17", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "flight to the hills", "note": "The commentary warns that Noah's son cannot save himself by choosing a mountain; in Sodom's destruction Lot is explicitly told to flee to the hills to escape being swept away, showing the mountain's safety depends on God's command."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ac52cb0732fd67bfd4a0", "ref": "WLC:Gen.19.22", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Zoar as granted refuge", "note": "The commentary says the house can be a place of safety; Lot is allowed to flee to Zoar, and destruction cannot begin until he arrives, making the small city a refuge by divine mercy."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a1e1d287d6a8571595ae", "ref": "WLC:Gen.41.35", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "צבר / ص ب ر; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's sabir stands with a group facing inescapable trouble; Joseph orders grain heaped up for famine, using Hebrew צבר for accumulation rather than the Arabic sense of patience or guarantorship."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7b2df8da2ab1199570da", "ref": "WLC:Gen.41.36", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "stored food prevents destruction", "note": "The commentary's communal protector averts loss in a no-exit calamity; Joseph specifies that stored food should keep the land from perishing during famine."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9b89b65a2ce132a3f1f4", "ref": "WLC:Gen.6.18", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Noah's household enters the ark", "note": "The commentary's Noah scene has a son excluded from the saved family; Genesis includes Noah, his sons, his wife, and his sons' wives in the covenantal rescue, a direct difference in who is sheltered."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ea0a1fd9c4b7ce5eb62", "ref": "WLC:Gen.7.1", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "come into ark, household", "note": "The commentary combines asylum and family as a root of safety; God calls Noah and all his household into the ark before judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fa004d2564576840cacc", "ref": "WLC:Gen.7.16", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "LORD shuts ark door", "note": "The commentary describes a refuge that truly protects from water; the LORD himself shuts Noah inside the ark as the flood begins."}
{"connection_id": "BC-083cee9883724f0c578b", "ref": "WLC:Gen.7.23", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "only those in the ark remain", "note": "The commentary rejects the son's mountain as protection from the flood; Genesis says all land creatures perish while Noah and those with him in the ark remain, making the ark rather than the mountain the refuge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-be3e343f90f8923361a1", "ref": "WLC:Gen.7.23", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Noah and companions survive", "note": "The commentary's false mountain fails to save Noah's son; Genesis describes Noah and those with him in the ark as the survivors, making the family outcome different."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0ef792ea5e445d30094f", "ref": "WLC:Gen.7.7", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Noah's sons enter ark", "note": "The commentary invokes the Qur'anic son who trusts a mountain and is lost; Genesis instead has Noah's sons enter the flood shelter with him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8e267d831e93440ac3a8", "ref": "WLC:Gen.8.4", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "ark rests on mountain", "note": "The commentary quotes the son's claim that a mountain will protect him from water; Genesis makes mountains the ark's eventual landing place, after the vessel has preserved its occupants."}
{"connection_id": "BC-74085c5d3c15337a8be2", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.4", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "אמנ; faithfulness and life", "note": "The commentary puts belief among the qualities that escape loss; Habakkuk says the righteous lives by his faithfulness, using the Hebrew אמנ root and tying faithfulness to life (the table correspondence is sound-based, not proof)."}
{"connection_id": "BC-89129090433678c9a9d3", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.4", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "אמונה / ء م ن; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary places believers outside the general loss; the righteous one lives by faithfulness amid the vision of judgment, a specific survival claim using אמנ."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f5d5e47679be47f3aba5", "ref": "WLC:Isa.10.1", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "חקק / ح ق ق; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's guarded truth has a public consequence; Isaiah condemns those who decree unjust decrees, using Hebrew חקק for the dangerous inversion of a protective rule."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b596c5b959b6bd4cad75", "ref": "WLC:Isa.16.4", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shelter outcasts from destroyer", "note": "The commentary cites asylum for a threatened outsider and safe passage; this appeal asks that outcasts be allowed to stay and sheltered from a destroyer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8c3566544f6bbc8a11c8", "ref": "WLC:Isa.25.4", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "refuge from storm", "note": "The commentary pictures a threatened person needing shelter; Isaiah calls God a refuge for the poor from storm and heat, specifying protection for the vulnerable."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7ab00f6741071b589164", "ref": "WLC:Isa.25.4", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "refuge for poor from storm", "note": "The commentary's shelter serves a distressed person; this hymn calls God a fortress for the poor and refuge from storm and heat."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f99cab0de4ed74c01c7", "ref": "WLC:Isa.26.1", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "safe city; salvation as wall", "note": "The commentary calls al-ṣalāḥ a name for a secure city; Isaiah's strong city has salvation appointed as its wall and rampart, making deliverance the city's protection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3c73d14a67345064dcfa", "ref": "WLC:Isa.26.1", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "strong city, salvation as walls", "note": "The commentary associates safety with a secure city; this song pictures salvation itself as that city's walls and ramparts."}

