{"connection_id": "BC-db8d6c930a2d0c0d134e", "ref": "WLC:Ps.91.10", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "calamity barred from tent", "note": "The commentary's secure house counters an inescapable disaster; the psalm promises that evil and plague will not approach the worshipper's tent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5bb7beb4d85d55c964a7", "ref": "WLC:Ps.91.2", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "refuge and fortress", "note": "The commentary makes asr a refuge to cling to; the speaker calls God his refuge and fortress, voicing the sought shelter directly."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e100d9b436145270d98f", "ref": "WLC:Ps.91.2", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "refuge, fortress, trust", "note": "The commentary joins shelter to faith as security from fear; the psalmist names God both fortress and the one in whom he trusts."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d9afac4a1dba9dce313e", "ref": "WLC:Ps.91.4", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "אמנ; faithfulness as shield and buckler", "note": "The commentary links iman with safety and also names armor as shelter; Psalm 91 calls God's faithfulness a shield and buckler, bringing trustworthiness and bodily protection together (the root match is a table sound correspondence, not proof)."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a8d688257748818cdf91", "ref": "WLC:Ps.91.4", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wings, shield, faithfulness", "note": "The commentary joins protective armor to truth that must be guarded; this verse makes divine faithfulness a shield under sheltering wings."}
{"connection_id": "BC-97ad8eedf1b7a12ac951", "ref": "WLC:Ps.91.9", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "refuge made into dwelling", "note": "The commentary defines the believer's safe place as a home; this verse says the worshipper has made the Most High his dwelling refuge."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e715a984a83fb8df4d0a", "ref": "WLC:Ruth.2.12", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "refuge under God's wings", "note": "The commentary calls asr a shelter for the endangered; Boaz blesses Ruth for coming to take refuge under the wings of Israel's God, using the same protective image for a vulnerable outsider."}
{"connection_id": "BC-859fbcfb548b4b28b63c", "ref": "WLC:Ruth.2.12", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "outsider seeks refuge under wings", "note": "The commentary joins safety, faith, and family belonging; Boaz says Ruth has left her old household and come to seek refuge under Israel's God's wings."}
{"connection_id": "BC-910ca28f23e27e34c1db", "ref": "WLC:Ruth.3.9", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "kinsman-redeemer spreads his wing", "note": "The commentary says a person's lineage can be a refuge; Ruth asks Boaz, her redeemer, to spread his wing over her, joining kinship protection to the sheltering image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f2ab9707fae6272e230a", "ref": "WLC:Ruth.3.9", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "kinsman asked to spread wing", "note": "The commentary's lineage becomes a refuge with a human guarantor beside the endangered; Ruth asks Boaz to spread his garment or wing over her because he is a family redeemer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-32b0475907cb38be859a", "ref": "WLC:Zech.8.16", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "truth spoken to one another", "note": "The commentary says people mutually counsel one another in truth; Zechariah commands each person to speak truth to a neighbor and render judgments that make for peace."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7146a450fd2584515cb2", "ref": "WLC:Zech.9.12", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "return to stronghold, prisoners of hope", "note": "The commentary reads the exception as arrival at safety; this verse summons prisoners of hope back to a fortress."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8c8ff2cd5e22387ef4c3", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.2.3", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seek righteousness to be hidden", "note": "The commentary's exception links right action with escape from destruction; the prophet tells the humble to seek righteousness and humility so they may be hidden on the day of wrath."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b32681d91fe3200b1f6e", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.3.12", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lowly remnant seeks refuge in name", "note": "The commentary reads belief as entry into a safe community; God leaves a humble people who take refuge in the LORD's name."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7853e50b7afe8e267020", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.3.13", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "truthful remnant rests without fear", "note": "The commentary joins guarded truth and freedom from fear in the saved group; the remnant neither lies nor acts unjustly and lies down with no one to frighten it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1cc546ed484a2c9f5e37", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 10:4", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Wisdom pilots Noah through the flood", "note": "The commentary contrasts a failed mountain refuge with rescue; Wisdom says divine Wisdom piloted the righteous man through the flood in a fragile vessel, interpreting the ark as God's guided shelter."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a47204279a77218f19f7", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 3:1", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "righteous souls safe in God's hand", "note": "The commentary describes loss as perishing; Wisdom says the souls of the righteous are in God's hand and beyond torment, preserving the counter-reading that apparent death is not final ruin."}
{"connection_id": "BC-74bcd83c4eb09494d78e", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 3:1", "target": "sec5", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "righteous souls held in God's hand", "note": "The commentary's secure hold faces apparent annihilation; Wisdom places righteous souls in God's hand beyond torment, making safety persist beyond physical death."}

